WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:06.520
Did you ever wonder if certain foods
have a direct effect on your health?

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.520
Well, I’m here to
solve that mystery

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.960
since you are
the foods you eat.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.480
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:24.120
Today we look at the role diet plays
in preventing and treating mood disorders.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.200
Depression affects more than
150 million people worldwide,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.320
making it a leading cause of
losing healthy years of life

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.523
as a result of disability.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.960
Why is depression so common?

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.760
Well, it is said “Nothing in
biology makes sense except

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.920
in the light of evolution,”

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.800
but why would be
evolve to get depressed?

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.840
Depression poses a baffling
evolutionary puzzle.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.320
It has such negative effects, but
remains so common and heritable,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.040
meaning a big chunk of risk is
passed down through our genes,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.040
so there must be some
kind of adaptive benefit.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.240
Otherwise, presumably, it would have
been naturally selected against.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:10.840
Maybe, depression is an evolutionary
strategy for defense against infection.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.200
Infection has been the
leading cause of mortality

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.120
throughout human history.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.640
The average life
expectancy was 25,

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.960
and it was not uncommon
for half our kids to die.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.360
With such stark capabilities,
infection has been a critical

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.520
and potent driving force
in natural selection.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.040
When we become infected, there
is a surge of inflammation

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.240
as our body mounts a counterattack,
and then what happens?

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.280
We feel lousy. We feel sick,
we get weak, tired, slow, and sleepy.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.160
We don’t see anyone;
we don’t want to do anything;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:43.680
all we want to do is sleep.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.600
It’s like we’re depressed—

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.560
and that’s great for
fighting infection.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.560
Not only does that help us
conserve energy so we can

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.520
put up a good fight,
but reduces social contact,

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.160
we’re not running around
infecting everyone.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:59.080
It’s the same reason we evolved
to think poop doesn’t smell good,

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.680
or decaying flesh.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.440
That keeps us safe
from infection.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.280
In fact, we see this phenomenon
with other social animals

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.760
like honeybees
and mole rats

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.560
who feel impelled to crawl off
and die alone when they get sick,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:15.000
which reduces the risk
to the rest of the community.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.520
The relationship between mental
health and inflammation

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.340
was first noted in 1887,
for which the only psychiatrist

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.440
to ever win the award
got a Nobel Prize.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.960
But what evidence have we
accumulated in the century since

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:33.040
that inflammation
causes depression?

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.040
Well, people who are depressed
have raised inflammatory markers

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.120
such as C-reactive protein.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.600
Inflammatory illnesses are associated
with greater rates of major depression.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.960
Indeed, that’s what’s found in
a variety of inflammatory conditions

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.000
including more benign
inflammatory conditions,

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.560
like as asthma
and allergies,

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.720
and that’s important,
suggesting the mood symptoms

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.800
are not simply ‘feeling bad
about having a terrible disease’

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.560
but may be directly tied
to the inflammation.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.440
Most powerfully, you can
actually induce depression

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:10.160
by inducing inflammation,
like when we give interferon

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.960
for certain cancers or
chronic infections—

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.320
up to 50% go on to suffer
major depression.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.240
Even just giving a vaccine
can cause enough inflammation

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.600
to trigger
depressive symptoms.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.000
Taken together, these studies
are strongly suggestive of

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.520
inflammation being a causative
factor of mood symptoms.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.560
So can an anti-inflammatory diet
help prevent depression?

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:34.800
We didn’t know…

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.200
until about 43,000 women without
depression were followed,

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.760
along with their diets,
for about a dozen years

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:45.000
to see who became depressed,
and it was those who at a more

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.000
inflammatory dietary pattern,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.800
characterized by more soda,
refined grains and meat,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.880
suggesting that chronic inflammation
may underlie the association

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.720
between diet
and depression.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:58.800
Normally, we think of omega-3’s
as anti-inflammatory,

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.600
but they found fish to
be pro-inflammatory,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.600
associated with increased C-reactive protein
levels consistent with recent findings

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:12.040
that omega-3’s don’t seem to help
with either depression or inflammation.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.520
The most anti-inflammatory
diet is plant-based,

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:22.680
which can cut C-Reactive Protein
levels by 30% within two weeks.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:27.240
Perhaps because of the anti-
inflammatory properties of antioxidants.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.600
I’ve talked about this before,
but never explained WHY

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.480
antioxidants are
anti-inflammatory.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.320
Oxidative damage caused by
free radicals may cause

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.000
an autoimmune response in the body
by changing the chemical structures

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.480
of otherwise ubiquitous molecules
to generate new structures

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.280
that the body
attacks as foreign.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.880
For example, when LDL
cholesterol gets oxidized,

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.400
our body creates antibodies
against it and attacks it.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:59.800
And so clinical depression can be
accompanied by increased oxidative stress

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.840
and the autoimmune inflammatory
responses it creates.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:07.400
Free radicals lead to
auto-immune inflammation.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.980
Where else does inflammation
come from in our diet?

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:15.760
Endotoxins. It’s worth reviewing how
the endotoxins in animal products

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.000
can cause a burst of inflammation
within hours of consumption.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.000
What does it
do to our mood?

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.360
If you inject endotoxin into people,
within a few hours, inflammation shoots up,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.840
and so does feelings
of depression,

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:36.280
as well as feelings of social
disconnection from people.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:39.960
Although previous research has
demonstrated that inflammatory activity

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.160
contributes to
depressive symptoms,

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.360
no work in humans has examined
the effect of experimentally induced

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.060
inflammation
on anhedonia—

00:05:48.098 --> 00:05:51.480
the lack of reaction to
pleasurable stimuli—

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.800
this is an an important
symptom of depression.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.960
No work has been done
that is....

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.760
until now.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.880
Within hours of endotoxin
hitting their bloodstream,

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:05.240
these experimental subjects not only
started to feel depressed,

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:07.880
but they had significant
reductions in activity

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:10.000
in the reward center
of the brain.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.080
They were less excited
about winning money

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.280
playing video games,
for example in the study.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:19.000
But by eliminating animal products,
and eating antioxidant rich diets,

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:24.520
we may be able to
prevent or treat depression.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:25.600
In our next story,

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.000
we look at how the natural enzyme
inhibitors in fruits and vegetables

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.360
may help explain the
improvement in mood

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:36.720
associated with switching
to a more plant-based diet.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.680
Why does frequent
consumption of vegetables

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.640
appear to cut ones odds of
depression by more than half?

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:45.760
And by more frequent they
mean eating vegetables

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:47.440
not 3 or more times a day,

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.880
but just 3 or more times a week.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.840
But even that seemed to cut
the odds of developing depression

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:59.520
by 60% after controlling for
a long list of variables.

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.960
In the 2012 study that found
that eliminating animal products

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.640
improved mood within 2 weeks,

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.320
the researchers blamed
arachidonic acid,

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:11.080
primarily in chicken and eggs,

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:13.280
which may adversely
impact mental health

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.960
via a cascade of
brain inflammation.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.320
But better moods on
plant-based diets

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.920
could also be from
the good stuff in plants,

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.480
a class of phytonutrients

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.520
that crosses the blood
brain barrier into our heads.

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:31.640
This recent review in the
journal Nutritional Neuroscience,

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:33.680
suggests that eating lots
of fruits and vegetables

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:37.640
"may present a noninvasive
natural and inexpensive"

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:41.000
"therapeutic means to
support a healthy brain."

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.400
Yeah, but how?

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:43.640
Well, to understand the latest,

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:46.120
we need to understand
the underlying biology,

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:49.640
the so-called monoamine
theory of depression -

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:51.160
the thought that
depression may arise

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:54.280
out of a chemical
imbalance in the brain.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:56.960
Here's the oversimplified version:

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:00.120
One of the ways that billions
of nerves in our brain

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:03.280
communicate with one another
is through chemical signals

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.330
called neurotransmitters.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:07.880
Note the two nerve cells
don't actually touch—

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:10.720
there's a physical
gap between them.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:12.800
To bridge that gap,
when one nerve wants

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:14.600
to tap the other
on the shoulder

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:16.480
it releases chemicals
into that gap,

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:18.800
including three monoamines:

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:23.160
serotonin, dopamine,
and norepinephrine.

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:25.560
These neurotransmitters
then float over

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.920
to the other nerve
to get its attention.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.400
The first nerve then
sucks them back up

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:34.320
to be reused the next
time it wants to talk,

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.280
but it's also constantly
manufacturing more,

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:42.000
and an enzyme, monoamine oxidase,

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.640
is constantly chewing them up

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:46.680
to maintain just the right amount.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:50.000
The way cocaine appears to work

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.640
is by acting as a monoamine
re-uptake inhibitor.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.560
It blocks the first nerve
from sucking back up

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.160
those three chemicals and so

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:00.360
there's this constant
tapping on the shoulder,

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:03.320
this constant signaling
to the next nerve.

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:05.040
Amphetamines work
in the same way,

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.160
but also increase
monoamine release.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:11.280
Ecstacy works like speed,

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.520
but just causes comparatively
more serotonin release.

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:18.240
After awhile, the next nerve

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.320
may be like "enough already!"

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.560
and down-regulate its receptors

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:24.000
to turn down the volume.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.120
It puts in ear plugs.
So you need more and more

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.440
of the drug to get
the same effect.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:31.240
Then when you are
not on the drug

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:34.680
you may feel crappy because
normal volume transmission

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:38.115
just isn't getting
through as much.

00:09:38.115 --> 00:09:41.360
Antidepressants are thought
to work along similar mechanisms.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:43.040
People who are depressed
appear to have

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.120
elevated levels of monoamine
oxidase in their brain.

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:51.442
That's the enzyme that breaks
down those neurotransmitters.

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:57.520
If the levels of your neurotransmitter-
eating enzyme is elevated,

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:01.040
then your levels of
neurotransmitters drops,

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:05.160
and you become depressed,
or so the theory goes.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:08.840
So a number of different classes
of drugs have been developed.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.240
The tricyclic antidepressants,

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:14.440
named because they have
three rings like a tricycle,

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:17.680
appear to block norepinephrine
and dopamine re-uptake,

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.760
and so even though your enzymes

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:22.960
may be eating these up
at an accelerated rate,

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:25.920
what gets released
sticks around longer.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.320
Then there came the
SSRIs like Prozac,

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:33.400
the selective serotonin
re-uptake inhibitors.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:34.480
Now you can know
what that means—

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:38.120
they just blocks the
re-uptake of serotonin.

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:40.000
Then there are drugs
that just block

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.360
the re-uptake of
norepinephrine,

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:46.200
or more dopamine
or the opposite.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:50.720
But if the problem is too much
high levels of monoamine oxidase,

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:52.760
why not just
block the enzyme?

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:55.640
Make a monoamine oxidase inhibitor—

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:58.080
and of course they did,

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.400
but they're considered
drugs of last resort

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:01.760
because of serious side effects,

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:05.280
not the least of which is
the dreaded "cheese effect,"

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:08.160
where eating certain
foods like certain cheeses

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:12.920
while on the drug can have
potentially fatal consequences.

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:15.200
If only there was a way
to tamp down the activity

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:16.920
of this enzyme without the whole

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:19.920
bleed-into-your-brain-thing
and die-thing.

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:22.360
Now we can finally talk
about the latest theory

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:26.920
as to why fruits and vegetables
may improve our mood.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:31.800
There are inhibitors of that depression-
associated enzyme in various plants.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:34.040
There are phytonutrients in spices,

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:36.640
such as cloves, oregano,
cinnamon, nutmeg,

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:38.440
but people don't eat enough spices

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:40.640
to get enough into the brain.

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:43.760
This dark green leafy has a lot,

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:45.400
but its name is tobacco,

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:47.240
which may actually
be one of the reasons

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:49.760
cigarettes make smokers
feel so good.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:53.760
OK, but what if you don't want
brain bleeds or lung cancer?

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:58.880
Well, there is a phytonutrient found
in apples, berries, and grapes,

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:03.240
and kale, onions, and green tea

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:10.720
that may indeed affect our brain
biology enough to improve our mood.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:11.640
Finally today,

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:16.440
the results of an interventional
trial of diet and mood.

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:20.880
A 2014 systematic review and
meta-analysis of dietary patterns

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:25.880
and depression concluded that a healthy
diet pattern was significantly associated

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:28.400
with the reduced odds of depression.

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:31.960
But out of the 21 studies they could
find in the medical literature,

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:35.760
they were only able to find one
randomized controlled trial,

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:39.600
considered the study design that
provides the highest level of evidence.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:43.200
And it was the study I profiled in
"Improving Mood Through Diet",

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:46.440
in which removing meat,
fish, poultry and eggs

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:50.800
improved several mood
scores in just two weeks.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:54.520
We've known those eating plant-based
tend to have healthier mood states”

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:59.200
less tension, anxiety, depression,
anger, hostility and fatigue,

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:01.120
but you couldn't tell if
it was cause and effect

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:03.920
until you put it to the test,
which they finally did.

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:07.600
But what could account
for such rapid results?

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:13.040
Well, eating vegetarian does give
you a better antioxidant status,

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:16.080
which may help with depression.

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:18.640
Also, as I've previously addressed,

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:22.320
how consumption of even a
single carbohydrate-rich meal

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:26.600
can improve depression, tension, anger,
confusion, sadness, fatigue, alertness,

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:32.640
and calmness scores among patients
with PMS, but what about long term?

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:36.720
Overweight men and women were
randomized into a low carb, high fat diet

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:40.240
or high carb, low fat diet for a year.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:44.720
By the end of the year, who had less
depression, anxiety, anger and hostility,

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:48.240
feelings of dejection,
tension, fatigue, better

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:51.251
vigor, less confusion
or mood disturbances?

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:55.200
These sustained improvements
in mood in the low fat group

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:56.560
compared with the low carb group

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:59.320
are consistent with results
from epidemiological studies

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:02.560
showing that diets high in carbohydrate
and low in fat and protein

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:05.460
are associated with lower
levels of anxiety and depression

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:09.800
and have beneficial effects
on psychological well-being.

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:14.600
The overall amount of fat in their diet
didn't change in this study, though.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:17.360
But the type of fat did.

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:22.360
Their arachidonic acid
intake fell to zero.

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:26.160
Arachidonic acid is an
inflammatory omega-6 fatty acid

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:30.560
that can adversely impact mental health
via a cascade of neuroinflammation.

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:33.520
It may inflame our brain.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:36.600
High blood levels in the bloodstream
have been associated

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:39.440
with a greater likelihood
of suicide risk, for example,

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:43.320
and major depressive episodes.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.600
How can we stay
away from the stuff?

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:48.160
Well, Americans are exposed
to arachidonic acid

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:52.640
primarily through
chicken and eggs.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:56.160
But when you remove chicken
and eggs, and other meat,

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:00.680
we can eliminate preformed
arachidonic acid from our diet.

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:04.240
So while high-quality treatment studies
investigating the impact of diet

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:08.640
on depression are scarce, there
is that successful 2-week trial,

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:13.200
but even better:
How about 22 weeks?

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:17.200
Overweight or diabetic employees
of a major insurance corporation

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:20.440
received either weekly group instruction
on a whole food plant-based diet

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:24.280
or no diet instruction
for five and a half months.

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:28.560
There was no portion size restriction,
no calorie counting, no carb counting.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.360
No change in exercise.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:34.040
No meals were provided, but the
company cafeteria did start offering

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:37.440
daily options such as lentil soup,
minestrone, bean burritos.

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:42.760
No meat, eggs, dairy, oil or junk, yet
they reported greater diet satisfaction

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:46.880
compared with the control group
participants who had no diet restrictions.

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:48.160
How'd they do though?

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:50.720
More participants in the
plant-based intervention group

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:55.600
reported improved digestion, increased
energy, better sleep than usual

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:59.000
at week 22 compared
with the control group.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:02.360
They also reported a significant
increase in physical functioning,

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:05.968
general health, vitality,
and mental health.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:09.640
There was also significant
improvements in work productivity,

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:13.320
thought to be due in large part
to their improvements in health.

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:18.520
So what this study demonstrated was
that a cholesterol-free diet is acceptable,

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:22.200
not only in research settings, but
in a typical corporate environment,

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:27.120
improving quality of life
and productivity at little cost.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:30.560
All we need now is a large,
randomized trial for confirmation,

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:35.200
but we didn't have
such a thing, until now.

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:39.360
Ten corporate sites across the country
from San Diego to Macon, Georgia.

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:41.200
Same kind of set up as before.

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:44.600
Can a plant-based nutrition program
in a multi-center, corporate setting

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:48.880
improve depression, anxiety,
and productivity?

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:53.680
Yes. Significant improvements
in depression, anxiety, fatigue,

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:57.400
emotional well being,
and daily functioning.

00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:59.840
Lifestyle interventions have
an increasingly apparent role

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:03.400
in physical and mental health,
and among the most effective

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:08.217
of these is the use
of plant-based diets.

